  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern this morning is defined by a\ntrough of low pressure over the Great Basin and a very strong \nridge of high pressure off the West Coast. The upper-ridge, \ncentered near 32N/130W, will continue to amplify today along the\nPacific Northwest and British Columbia coastlines, then flop \ninto the Pacific Northwest Thursday. This will essentially \nresult in a SW-NE orientation of the ridge axis which will help \ndrive an anomously strong surface high southward through the \nIntermountain West and into the southern Rockies by 16/00Z \nThursday afternoon. The 14/00Z GFS and ECMWF ensemble runs are \ntrending even stronger with this surface high, sitting 3.5-4.5 \nstandard deviations from the norm along the SE AZ/SW NM border \nby 16/21Z Friday afternoon. \n\nA closed upper-low that develops over WRN AZ Thursday afternoon\nwill help pull this strong surface high westward into Southeast\nArizona Thursday night into Friday. The ensemble members of the\nGFS and ECMWF have been consistent with the evolution of this \nsolution over the past several days, with little variance. \n\nModel guidance continues to indicate 850 mb winds between 45-55\nkts across our CWA between 16/06Z Friday morning thru 17/06Z \nFriday evening, with winds remaining elevated between 35-45 kts \nthrough 17/18Z Saturday. This prolonged period of strong \neasterly flow, combined with a very tight surface pressure \ngradient, increases confidence in a significant and potentially \nlong-duration wind event across Southeast Arizona. \n\nThe 14/00Z GFS deterministic model still indicated strong static\nstability near and immediately above ridge-top level. This, combined\nwith the persistent strong easterly cross-barrier flow appears \nfavorable for mountain-wave development...with potentially \nsignificant downslope wind acceleration along the lee slopes of \nthe higher terrain (west side of the higher terrain in this \nparticular case). Although the strongest winds may be focused \nimmediately downstream of the higher terrain, terrain gaps and \neast-west oriented valleys will also be susceptible to strong \nwinds given the magnitude and duration of the low-level pressure\ngradient.\n\nConfidence is high Wind Advisory criteria will be met both Friday\nand Saturday, with sustained ELY/SELY winds of 30-35 mph and \ngusts of 50-60 mph. Given the potential for mountain-wave \namplification, there is increasing concern that portions of the \narea could experience sustained winds exceeding 40 mph with \ngusts in excess of 60 mph which would put it into High Wind \nWarning criteria. The 14/01Z NBM probability for wind gusts in \nexcess of 60 mph is 30-60% across southern Cochise, SE Pima and \nSanta Cruz counties between 16/09Z-16/18Z Friday, with a \nsecondary maxima across Graham and southern Greenlee counties \nbetween 17/00Z and 17/09Z Friday night. I will punt the decision\nto upgrade the current Wind Advisory to the dayshift with another\nmodel run under their belts.",
